  "image_name": "1ff930c0b05f4c6183fae8a24fb53405.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares four extracorporeal bioartificial liver support systems, all designed to temporarily treat liver failure by circulating a patient’s blood outside the body through purification and cell-based support modules. In every diagram, blood or separated plasma is drawn from the patient, routed through devices that remove toxins, and then returned.\n\nA central theme is the different ways these systems combine detoxification with bioreactors containing living liver-related cells. HepatAssist appears to use plasma separation plus a charcoal column and a bioreactor. ELAD routes plasma through multiple bioreactors, with added control elements such as a glucose pump and heat exchanger. The modular extracorporeal liver support design separates functions most explicitly, showing a plasma path connected to an oxygenated cell module and a distinct detox module, along with dialysis-related filters and solutions. The AMC-bioartificial liver also integrates plasma processing, oxygenation, filtration, and a bioreactor in a somewhat more compact circuit.\n\nOverall, the image highlights that these devices share the same clinical goal—external liver replacement support—but differ in circuit architecture and in how they balance plasma filtration, adsorption or dialysis-based detoxification, oxygenation, and bioreactor-based metabolic assistance.",
